@using BedrockSentry.Core.Instances

@* What happened to a removed instance, shown on the Status page the removal sends
   you to (`T169`).

   Moved here out of ConfigurationTab rather than copied. That panel used to render
   this itself, below the fold of a page describing a server that no longer existed,
   and leaving its copy behind would be two renderings of one report with only one of
   them reachable — the shape `T155` was.

   ErrorBanner is the precedent for a small single-purpose component a page renders
   once. *@

@* T186. The palette and the ARIA role follow the outcome, because one of the arms
   reports irreversible loss and the rest do not. Both read Outcome.AnythingLost
   rather than switching again here — the rule lives in Core where the suite can
   reach it, and there is no second copy to drift. *@
<div class="page-notice @(Outcome.AnythingLost ? "page-notice-loss" : null)"
     role="@(Outcome.AnythingLost ? "alert" : "status")">
    <p><strong>@Outcome.Name</strong> has been removed.</p>
    <ul class="milestone-list">
        <li>
            @* `T176`. The PartlyDeleted arm is the reason this is a switch over three
               states rather than a bool: a delete that got part-way used to land in
               the "kept" branch, so world data was lost and this sentence said it was
               not. That branch now says so first and loudest, because it is the only
               one an administrator has to act on. *@
            @switch (Outcome.Files)
            {
                case DirectoryRemoval.Deleted:
                    <text>Its files and world were deleted from <code>@Outcome.WorkingDirectory</code>.</text>
                    break;

                case DirectoryRemoval.PartlyDeleted:
                    <text>
                        <strong>Deleting its files failed part-way, so some of the world is
                        already gone.</strong> @DirectoryRemovalPhrase.OpeningSentence(Outcome.FilesFailedBecause)
                        Check <code>@Outcome.WorkingDirectory</code> before relying on
                        anything left in it — what is still there is what could not be
                        deleted, not a part of the world the delete never reached.
                    </text>
                    break;

                case DirectoryRemoval.Refused:
                    <text>
                        Its files were <strong>kept</strong>, untouched:
                        @DirectoryRemovalPhrase.Sentence(Outcome.FilesFailedBecause)
                        Delete <code>@Outcome.WorkingDirectory</code> by hand if you meant to.
                    </text>
                    break;

                default:
                    <text>Its files are still at <code>@Outcome.WorkingDirectory</code>.</text>
                    break;
            }

            @* `T190`. The console transcripts are part of what "delete its files"
               promises, so a failure there has to reach the operator — but they are
               log files, not world data, and they get a trailing clause on this line
               rather than a bullet of their own or a share of the sentence above.

               They used to be reported by overwriting Files with PartlyDeleted. That
               could only fire when the working directory had been deleted COMPLETELY,
               so the operator was told some of the world was gone and sent to inspect
               a folder that no longer existed. No path existed on which that was true.
               Only the failure is shown: "the logs were deleted too" is not news.

               `T192`. IT NAMES ITS OWN FOLDER, and that is the whole point of the
               <code> below. This arm used to print no path at all, so the only path in
               the notice was the working directory — which on this branch was deleted
               SUCCESSFULLY — and a reader following the sentence went to the wrong
               folder. The transcript root is rendered nowhere else in the panel, has no
               Storage override and sits under the ACL-denied data root, so if this
               sentence does not name it the operator has no route to it at all.

               Not merely a missing nicety: `T190`'s severity bound called that defect a
               wording problem BECAUSE the path was printed mid-message, and `T190`'s own
               fix is what deleted the fragment that printed it.

               `Refused` IS DELIBERATELY NOT TESTED HERE, where it used to be. The
               transcripts are assigned only from TryDeleteDirectory, whose four returns
               do not include Refused, so that arm could never fire — and its wording
               would have been wrong if it had, because Refused means nothing was
               touched. InstanceCreationTests.A_transcript_removal_never_reports_refused
               pins that unreachability across both ways the delete can end, so adding a
               refusing path fails the suite rather than silently re-enabling a sentence
               that contradicts it. *@
            @if (Outcome.Transcripts is DirectoryRemoval.PartlyDeleted)
            {
                <text>
                    Its console transcripts at <code>@Outcome.TranscriptDirectory</code>
                    were not all deleted, which does not affect the world or the archives:
                    @DirectoryRemovalPhrase.Sentence(Outcome.TranscriptsFailedBecause)
                </text>
            }
        </li>
        <li>
            @* Missing until `T169`: the service has always worked this out and logged
               it, and only the panel was silent — about the one step `D39` calls
               irreversible.

               The last branch deliberately says only that nothing was deleted. From
               the outcome alone, "you did not ask" and "you asked and there was no
               backup folder" are the same pair of values, and the service is right
               not to claim a deletion for a folder that never existed. Do not reword
               this to "you chose to keep them" — that is false in the second case. *@
            @switch (Outcome.Backups)
            {
                case DirectoryRemoval.Deleted:
                    <text>Its backup archives were deleted.</text>
                    break;

                case DirectoryRemoval.PartlyDeleted:
                    <text>
                        <strong>Deleting its backup archives failed part-way, so some of
                        them are already gone.</strong> @DirectoryRemovalPhrase.OpeningSentence(Outcome.BackupsFailedBecause)
                        Check the backups folder — the archives still there are the ones
                        that could not be deleted, not the ones it chose to keep.
                    </text>
                    break;

                case DirectoryRemoval.Refused:
                    <text>
                        Its backup archives were <strong>kept</strong>, untouched:
                        @DirectoryRemovalPhrase.Sentence(Outcome.BackupsFailedBecause)
                    </text>
                    break;

                default:
                    <text>No backup archives were deleted.</text>
                    break;
            }
        </li>
        <li>
            @(Outcome.PlayerRecordsForgotten > 0
                ? $"{Outcome.PlayerRecordsForgotten} player records were erased."
                : "No player records were stored for it, so none were erased.")
        </li>
        @* `T400`. Only when asked, and a failure says what is still open. *@
        @if (Outcome.FirewallRules is { } rules)
        {
            <li>
                @if (rules.Succeeded)
                {
                    <text>Firewall: @rules.Message</text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>
                        <strong>Its firewall rules were not removed:</strong> @rules.Message
                        Any inbound rule named "BedrockSentry - @Outcome.Name (…)" still
                        holds its port open; delete it in Windows Defender Firewall.
                    </text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
    <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="OnDismiss">Dismiss</button>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public InstanceRemovalOutcome Outcome { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <remarks>
    /// The holder has already been emptied by the time this renders, so dismissing
    /// only clears the page's own copy — there is nothing left to take again.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnDismiss { get; set; }
}
